1.	Design a payroll management system for an organization with different types of employees.
Create an abstract class Employee with private fields id (int), name (String), and department (String). Include a constructor, getters, and an abstract method double calculateSalary().
Create two subclasses:
FullTimeEmployee: Adds baseSalary (double) and bonus (double). calculateSalary() returns baseSalary + bonus.
ContractEmployee: Adds hourlyRate (double) and hoursWorked (int). calculateSalary() returns hourlyRate * hoursWorked.
Create a PayrollManager class that maintains a List<Employee> and implements the following methods using Java Streams and Lambdas:
Map<String, Double> getDepartmentWiseTotalSalary(): Returns a map of each department to its total salary payout.
List<String> getTopEarners(double threshold): Returns a sorted list (alphabetical) of employee names whose calculated salary is strictly greater than the threshold.
Constraints
1 ≤ Number of employees ≤ 10⁴
Salaries and rates are non-negative doubles.
Sample Input & Output
Input:
FullTimeEmployee(101, "Alice", "IT", 60000, 10000) -> Salary: 70000.0
ContractEmployee(102, "Bob", "IT", 500, 100) -> Salary: 50000.0
FullTimeEmployee(103, "Charlie", "HR", 55000, 5000) -> Salary: 60000.0
threshold = 55000
Output:
Department-wise Total Salary: {IT=120000.0, HR=60000.0}
Top Earners (> 55000): ["Alice", "Charlie"]



2.	Create a digital library catalog that prevents duplicate entries and analyzes book borrowing metrics.
Define an interface Searchable with a method boolean matchesKeyword(String keyword).
Create a Book class implementing Searchable with isbn (String), title (String), author (String), and borrowCount (int).
Two books are considered identical if they have the same isbn.
matchesKeyword(String keyword) returns true if title or author contains the keyword (case-insensitive).
Create a LibraryCatalog class storing books in a Set<Book> with methods using Streams:
List<Book> searchBooks(String keyword): Returns all books matching the keyword, sorted by borrowCount in descending order.
Optional<Book> getMostBorrowedBook(): Returns the book with the highest borrowCount.
Constraints
0 ≤ borrowCount ≤ 10⁶
ISBN strings are non-empty and unique per distinct edition.
Sample Input & Output
Input:
Add Book("978-0134685991", "Effective Java", "Joshua Bloch", 150)
Add Book("978-0134685991", "Effective Java 3rd Ed", "Joshua Bloch", 200) (Ignored as duplicate ISBN)
Add Book("978-0596009205", "Head First Java", "Kathy Sierra", 320)
Call searchBooks("java")
Output:
Search Results: [Head First Java (320), Effective Java (150)]
Most Borrowed: Head First Java
